    "completeness_proof": "Put pi=sqrt(2) and epsilon=1+pi.  The coefficient epsilon is a unit and N(E6^4) is even unimodular.  For a primitive r=(x,y), reflection integrality says q(r) divides 2I(r), where I(r)=(epsilon*x,(y,N)). Away from (pi), local unimodularity and primitivity make I(r) the unit ideal.  At (pi), a unit control gives v_pi(q)=0; otherwise a primitive spatial coordinate makes I(r) a unit, while evenness and q|2I force v_pi(q)=2.  Since every totally-positive unit of Z[sqrt(2)] is a square, the complete canonical positive norms are 1 and 2."
  },
